  <h1>IVD Testing Methodologies</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Rapid tests use one of two methodologies: colloidal-gold lateral flow (visual, qualitative) or fluorescence immunoassay (analyzer-read, quantitative). Lateral flow is instrument-free and lower-cost; FIA adds numeric results.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Colloidal-gold lateral flow</th><th>Fluorescence immunoassay (FIA)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Readout</th><td>Visual line (naked eye)</td><td>Fluorescent signal (analyzer-read)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Result type</th><td>Qualitative / semi-quantitative</td><td>Quantitative</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Instrument</th><td>None</td><td>Fluorescence analyzer required</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Principle (lateral flow)</h2>
  <p>
    A labeled antibody captures the target analyte on a nitrocellulose membrane as the sample migrates
    by capillary action, producing a visible test line within minutes.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/products/">Browse products by methodology →</a></p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>What is the difference between lateral flow and FIA?</strong> Lateral flow gives a visual, qualitative result; FIA uses a fluorescent label read by an analyzer for quantitative output.</p>
  </div>

